     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times a day adds up to 5100 mg a day. The usual adult maximum is 2550 mg a day for immediate-release tablets and 2000 mg a day for extended-release. This order is double the highest recommended amount, which raises the risk of lactic acidosis, a rare but potentially fatal side effect, and of serious stomach and bowel problems. Normal kidney function and body weight don't change that limit. The number may be a transcription error. For example, the intended order might have been 850 mg three times daily (2550 mg a day) or 1700 mg total a day split into doses.\n\n**2. Only a licensed prescriber can authorize the order.** You mentioned you're not a clinician. Medication orders need to come from a prescriber, such as the attending physician, a resident or an advanced practice provider, under their own name. That isn't a judgment about your role. It's about who is legally and clinically responsible for the order, and it lets pharmacy and nursing follow up with the right person.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- Ask the patient's prescribing team to confirm the intended dose and enter or authorize the order. The medicine service's attending or the on-call resident would be the right contact.\n- If you're passing along a verbal or written instruction from a prescriber, please have that person check the dose, since the current one may be an error.\n\nOnce a prescriber confirms a corrected dose, I can help them enter it."
      }
     ],
