     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order, even with your confirmation. Confirming doesn't fix either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still dangerous.** 5100 mg a day is twice the maximum, and entering it as written could seriously harm the patient. No one should approve that dose without a documented clinical reason, and I'm not aware of one.\n- **It still needs a prescriber.** As a non-clinician, you can't authorize a medication order, whatever your position. This is a patient-safety and regulatory requirement, not a matter of seniority.\n\n**To move forward:**\n1. Contact the patient's attending physician or the on-call medicine resident and tell them about the dose problem.\n2. Have the prescriber confirm the intended dose. It may have been meant as 850 mg three times daily, or 1700 mg total per day.\n3. The prescriber can then enter the order themselves or with my help. Pharmacy can also check the dose.\n\nIf something urgent is happening with this patient's care, the fastest route is to page the medicine team directly. I'm happy to help once a prescriber is involved."
      }
     ],
